We formalize these requirements as follows.

::: {#def-compatible-prices}
# Compatible Prices 
Given a matching $M$, a pricing of the vertices, $p: V \to \R$, is said to be a _$M$-compatible pricing_ if 

i. for all unmatched vertices $v \in L$ we have $p(v) = 0$;
ii. for all edges $e$ we have $c_e^p \ge 0$;
iii. for all edges $e \in M$ we have $c_e^p = 0$.
:::

We first show that a perfect matching $M$ along with a $M$-compatible pricing $p$ easily certify the optimality of $M$.

::: {#lem-pricing-certify}
# Compatible Prices Certify Optimality
Let $M$ be a matching with $|M| = k$. If there exists a $M$-compatible pricing $p$, then $M$ is a min-cost matching of cardinality $k$.
:::
::: {.proof key=@lem-pricing-certify}

Let $M'$ be any other matching of cardinality $k$. Note that $M \oplus M'$ is a union of alternating components. We then have, 
\begin{align*}
c^p(M') &= c^p(M) + \Delta^p_M(M \oplus M') \\
&= c^p(M) + c^p(M' \backslash M) - c^p(M \backslash M') \\
&= c^p(M' \backslash M) \\ 
&\ge c^p(M) = 0.
\end{align*}
:::

In summary, compatible pricings have two key offerings: (a) it allows us to efficiently compute an augmenting path of minimum cost and (b) it alows us to easily certify the optimality of a perfect matching. 

This leads to a natural candidate for an algorithm: at every iteration, we augment by the min-cost path for the current pricing and afterword, modify the pricing to be compatible with respect to the new matching. Note that initially when our matching is $M = \emptyset$, the zero pricing $p = 0$ is $M$-compatible. The only task remaining is to come up with a mechanism for maintaining compatibility of the pricing. 

::: {#lem-update}
# Maintaining Compatibility
Let $M$ be a matching, let $p$ be a $M$-compatible pricing and let $M'$ be a matching obtained by augmenting along the augmenting path minimizing $\Delta_M^p$. By $d_M^p(v)$ denote the min-cost of a path from the unmatched set $L \backslash V(M)$ to $v$. 

Then $p'(v) = d_{M}^p (v) + p(v)$ is a compatible set of prices for $M'$.
:::
::: {.proof key=@lem-update}
Consider each of the requirements, 

i. for all unmatched vertices $v \in L$ we have $p'(v) = 0$

Suppose $v \in L \backslash V(M)$. Then $d^p_M (v) = 0$. Thus, $p'(v) = p(v) + d^p_M(v) = 0$.

ii. for all edges $e$ we have $c_e^{p'} \ge 0$

Consdier $e = (u, v)$ and then, 

\begin{align*}
d^p_M (v) &\le d^p_M (u) + c^p_e \\
\implies 0 &\le d^p_M (u) + c^p_e - d^p_M (v) \\
&= d^p_M (u) + p(u) + c_e - (p(v) + d^p_M(v)) \\
&= p'(u) + c_e - p'(v) \\
&= c^{p'}_e.
\end{align*}

iii. for all edges $e \in M'$ we have $c_e^{p'} = 0$

Suppose $e = (u, v) \in M$. The only edge entering $u$ in $G_M$ is the directed edge $(v, u)$. Thus, 
\begin{align*}
d_M^p(u) &= d^p_M(v) - c^p_e  \\
\implies 0 &= d_M^p(u) + c^p_e - d_M^p(v)  \\
&= p'(u) + c_e - p'(v) \\
&= c^{p'}_e.
\end{align*}

Now, suppose $e = (u, v) \in M' \backslash M$. Note that these edges are along the minimum-$\Delta_M^p$ augmenting paths in $G_M$. Thus, 
\begin{align*}
d^p_M(v) &= d^p_M(u) + c^p_e \\
\implies 0 &= d^p_M(u) + c^p_e - d^p_M(v) \\
&= p'(u) + c_e - p'(v) \\
&= c_e^{p'}.
\end{align*}

:::

We summarize the entire section by formally presenting the algorithm.

```pseudocode
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Pricing Algorithm for Min-Cost Perfect Matching}
\begin{algorithmic}
    \State{\textbf{initialize} $M = \emptyset$, $p = 0$}
    \While{$M$ is not a perfect matching}
    \State{$P \leftarrow$ \textbf{find} an $M$-augmenting path minimizing $\Delta_M^p(\cdot)$}
    \State{$d^p_M(v) \leftarrow$ min-cost of a path $\set{L \backslash V(M)} \to v$}
    \State{$M \leftarrow M \oplus P$}
    \State{$p \leftarrow d^p_M + p$}
    \EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
```

In each iteration, computing $d^p_M$ takes $\mathcal{O}(m \log n)$ time via Dijkstra's and computing $P$ takes an additional $\mathcal{O}(n)$. There are at most $n/2$ iterations to reach a perfect matching. Thus, the overall runtime is $\mathcal{O}(mn \log n + n^2)$.

#### LP Relaxation of Bipartite Matching

The algorithm in the prior section is quite elegant but appears to come from nowhere. In this section, we present a more principled way of designing the very same algorithm.

We start off by relaxing our notion of a matching.

::: {#def-frac-match}
# Fractional Matching

A _fractional matching_ in a bipartite graph $(L, R, E)$ is a vector $\vv{x} = (x_{uv})_{\{u, v\} \in E}$ that satisfies 
\begin{align*}
\forall u \in L,&& \sum_v &x_{uv} \le 1,  \\
\forall v \in R,&& \sum_u &x_{uv} \le 1, \\
\forall \{u, v\} \in E,&& \quad &x_{uv} \ge 0.
\end{align*}

A _fractional perfect matching_ additionally satisfies 
\begin{align*}
\forall u \in L,&& \sum_v &x_{uv} = 1,  \\
\forall v \in R,&& \sum_u &x_{uv} = 1.
\end{align*}
:::

Restricting each $x_{uv} \in \{0, 1\}$ to be integral allows us to interpret $\vv{x}$ as an indicator vector for a set of edges $M \subseteq E$. The fractional matching constraints require $M$ to be a matching and the fractional perfect matching constraints require $M$ to be a perfect matching. This is the sense in which allowing $x_{uv} \in [0, 1]$, "relaxes" our discrete notion of a matching.

::: {#lem-relaxation}
# Integral Matching
Suppose $\vv{x} \in \bits^{E}$. If $\vv{x}$ is a fractional matching then it is a matching. Furthermore, if $\vv{x}$ is a perfect fractional matching then it is a perfect matching.
:::
:::{.proof}
:::

Relaxing enlarges our feasible set and one may be concerned that properties of a relaxation may not represent the properties of the original objects we were studying. 

::: {#exm-non-bipartite}
# Non-Bipartite Relaxation
In our definition for fractional matching, we required the underlying graph to be bipartite. If we allowed for non-bipartite graphs, one doesn't need to look beyond $K_3$ to see possible problems that may arise. 

Indeed, the illustrated example we have a fractional matching where each edge has a positive value. This fractional matching appears to be "larger" than any actual matching availible in the underlying graph. 
:::

Fortunately, the following theorem guarantees a nice correspondence between matchings and fractional matchings for bipartite graphs.

::: {#prp-birk-neuman}
# Birkhoff-von Neumann
In any bipartite graph, every fractional perfect matching is a convex combination of integral perfect matchings. 

That is, for any fractional perfect matching $\vv{x} \in [0, 1]^E$ there exists a finite collection of integral perfect matchings $\vv{y}_i \in \bits^E$ such that 
\begin{align*}
\vv{x} = \sum_{i} \alpha_i \vv{y}_i
\end{align*}
where $\alpha_i \ge 0$ and $\sum_i \alpha_i = 1$.
:::

::: {.proof key=@prp-birk-neuman}
:::

We are interested in the min-cost fractional perfect matching problem.

**Minimum-Cost Fractional Perfect Matching.** Given an undirected bipartite graph $G = (L, R, E)$ together with costs $c: E \to \R_{\ge 0}$ for each edge, find a fractional perfect matching $\vv{x}$ that minimizes $c(\vv{x}) = \sum_{\{u, v\} \in E} c_{uv} x_{uv}$.

Alternatively, we can express this problem as a _linear program_, dubbed the _primal_.

\begin{align*}
& \text{minimize} &&\sum_{\{u, v\} \in E} c_{uv} x_{uv} \\
& \text{subject to} &&\sum_{v} x_{uv} = 1, &\forall u \in L, \\
& &&\sum_{v} x_{uv} = 1, &\forall v \in R, \\
& && x_{uv} \ge 0, &\forall \{u, v\} \in E.
\end{align*}

By the Birkhoff-von Neumann theorem, we note that the objective value of this problem is the same as the objective value for its integral perfect matching counterpart.

::: {#cor-equivalence}
\begin{align*}
\text{cost}(\text{min-cost fractional perfect matching}) = \text{cost}(\text{min-cost integral perfect matching})
\end{align*}
:::

::: {.proof key=@cor-equivalence}
:::

We start by first considering how we can prove lower bounds on the optimum. A nice trick is to take linear combinations of the constraints of our primal program to "algebraically reconstruct" the objective function. More precisely, for every vertex $u \in L$ and $v \in R$, define variables $y_u, y_v$ by which we scale the corresponding degree constraints,

\begin{align*}
\sum_v y_u x_{uv} = y_u, \quad \sum_u y_v x_{uv} = y_v.
\end{align*}

Summing these equations we obtain 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{(u, v) \in L \times R} (y_u + y_v) x_{uv} = \sum_{u \in L} y_u + \sum_{v \in R} y_v.
\end{align*}

If we have $y_u + u_v \le c_{uv}$ for every $(u, v) \in L \times R$ then we have the lower bound on the optimum,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{\{u,v\} \in E} c_{uv} x_{uv} \ge \sum_{(u, v) \in L \times R} (y_u + y_v) x_{uv} = \sum_{u \in L} y_u + \sum_{v \in R} y_v.
\end{align*}

We can represent the strongest lower bound obtainable via this method as a solution to a different linear program, dubbed the _dual_.

\begin{align*}
& \text{maximize} && \sum_{w \in L \sqcup R} y_w \\
& \text{subject to} && y_u + y_v \le c_{uv}, &\forall \{u, v\} \in E, \\
& && y_w \in \R, &\forall w \in L \sqcup R.
\end{align*}

Our prior discussion motivating the dual corresponds to a special case of _weak duality_.  

::: {#prp-weak-dual}
# Weak Duality for Min-Cost Fractional Matching

For any primal-feasible $\vv{x}$ and dual-feasible $\vv{y}$ we have, 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{w \in L \sqcup R} y_w \le \sum_{\{u, v\} \in E} c_{uv} x_{uv}.
\end{align*}
:::

