### _Mise en Place_ : Ski-Rental Problem

We now take a brief excursion from discussing matching to introduce online algorithms and primal-dual approaches for such problems. We will take the ski-rental problem as being a prototypic example for this model.

**Ski-Rental Problem.** A skier will make $T$ ski trips this season. Each trip they must either rent a ski, for a cost of $1$ per trip, or buy a ski, for a cost of $B$ the first time you choose this option, and $\emptyset$ after. The goal is minimize total cost.

If $T$ is known, there is an easy solution. 
\begin{align*}
\begin{cases}
\, \text{buy a ski at } t = 0 &\text{if } T > B, \\
\, \text{rent a ski at all timesteps } t &\text{if } T \le B.
\end{cases}
\end{align*}

In the _online_ version, $T$ is unknown. The common benchmark for online problems is a comparison with respect to the offline optimal.

::: {#def-online-min}
# Competitiveness for Minimization Problems
An online algorithm for a minimization problem is _$c$-competitive_ if there exists a constant $b$ such that for all input sequences,  
\begin{align*}
\alg \le c \cdot \opt + b
\end{align*}
where $\alg$ and $\opt$ denote the value of the algorithm's solution and the optimum one, respectively. It is _strictly competitive_ if $b = 0$. A randomized algorithm is _$c$-competitive_ (against an oblivious adversary) if the above holds with $\E[\alg]$ in place of $\alg$ .
:::

We start off by presenting a simple deterministic algorithm that just rents as much as possible until it is abundantly clear that it should have just bought the ski. 

```pseudocode
#| html-line-number: false

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{A Deterministic Algorithm for Ski-Rental}
\begin{algorithmic}
\If{$t \le B$}
\State{\textbf{rent} a ski.}
\Else
\State{\textbf{buy} a ski.}
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
```

This algorithm turns out to be strictly $2$-competitive.

::: {#prp-deterministic-has-2-comp}
# 2-Competitive
The algorithm given above is strictly $2$-competitive.
:::
::: {.proof key=@prp-deterministic-has-2-comp}

We can do case analysis on $T$. If $T \le B$ then $\opt = T = \alg$. If $T > B$ then $\alg = 2 B$ whereas $\opt = B$. Thus, in both cases we have $\alg \le 2 \opt$.

:::

In fact, no deterministic algorithm can beat this guarantee.

::: {#prp-deterministic-2-comp-opt}
# Optimality
No deterministic algorithm for the ski-rental problem can be $c$-competitive for $c < 2$.
:::
::: {.proof key=@prp-deterministic-2-comp-opt

A deterministic algorithms rents a ski if and only if $t < K$ for some $K \in \N \cup \{\infty\}$.  

If $K = \infty$, the adversary constructs an instance where $T = 2 B$. Then $\alg = 2 B$ whereas $\opt = B$. Thus, the competitive ratio here is $\alg/\opt = 2$

If $K < \infty$, the adversary starts by setting $T = K$. If $B > K$ then $\alg = K - 1 + B \ge 2 K$  whereas $\opt = T = K$. If $B < K$ then $\alg = K - 1 + B \ge 2 B$ whereas $\opt = B$. Regardless, the competitive ratio here is also $\alg/\opt \ge 2$.

:::

The next natural question would be to wonder how randomization could possible improve this. To answer this, we will again consider an LP relaxation of our problem-- this time interpreting the relaxed solution as a probability distribution. The following primal, parametrized on $T$, is our relaxation.

\begin{align*}
& \text{minimize} && \sum_{i = 1}^T x_i + B z \\
& \text{subject to} && x_i + z \ge 1, &\forall i \in [T]\\
& && x_1, \dots, x_T, z \ge 0.
\end{align*}

We take its dual, 

\begin{align*}
& \text{maximize} && \sum_{i = 1}^T y_i \\
& \text{subject to} && y_i \le 1, &\forall i \in [T],\\
& && \sum_{i = 1}^T y_i \le B, \\
& && y_i \ge 0, &\forall i \in [T].
\end{align*}

Both of these are easy linear programs to solve. This isn't necessarily the point though-- solving the primal LP requires us to know $T$ and we already have a clean characterization of the offline solution. Rather, the idiomatic point of considering LP relaxations for the design of algorithms is to lighten our cognitive load. In practice, reasoning about a primal and dual program often naturally helps us understand the structure of the underlying problem. 

In this case, the dual problem is much nicer. In fact, it isn't too difficult to solve it online, without prior knowledge of $T$. As each variable $y_i$ "arrives" online, we can just set it maximally to $y_i = 1$. The second constraint then gives us that we only need to do this while $i \le B$ and then we must set $y_i = 0$ for all $i > B$. These constraints also guarantees the optimality of our approach.

::: {#lem-optimal-dual}
An optimal dual solution is given by, 
\begin{align*}
y_i = \begin{cases}
1 &\text{if } i \le B,\\
0 &\text{if } i > B.
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
:::
::: {.proof}
:::

Now, our goal is to go from this online algorithm for the dual problem to a randomized algorithm for the ski-rental problem. Our strategy is outlined as follows.

+ Set $\{x_i, z\}$ and $\{y_i\}$ in tandem. Note that $x_t, y_t$ will be permenantly set at time $t$. We note that $z$ will only increase in time-- as we will see, it is only schemes that guarantee this property that naturally correspond to a randomized online algorithm. 

+ For all $t \le T$, make sure $(x_1, \dots, x_t, z)$ is feasible for $\textsc{P}{RIMAL}(t)$, and $(y_1, \dots, y_t)$ is feasible for $\textsc{D}{UAL}(t)$.

+ Make sure the $\textsc{P}{RIMAL}$ objective never exceeds the $\textsc{D}{UAL}$ objective by more than a factor of $\alpha < \infty$.

Note that, by weak duality, we must have $1 \le \frac{\textsc{P}{RIMAL}}{\textsc{D}{UAL}} < \alpha$.

If we set a value of $x_i$ (the algorithm we design will have $\le x_{i - 1}$) and set $z = 1 - x_i \ge 1 - x_{i - 1}$ to satisfy the primal constraint $z + x_i \ge 1$ without increasing the objective value unnecessarily. Then $\Delta \textsc{P}{RIMAL} = x_i + B \Delta z = x_i + B (x_{i - 1} - x_i)$ and $\Delta \textsc{D}{UAL} = \begin{cases}1 &\text{if } i \le B \\ 0 &\text{if } i > B\end{cases}$. We further want to guarantee 
\begin{align*}
\Delta \textsc{P}{RIMAL} \le \alpha \cdot \Delta \textsc{D}{UAL}.
\end{align*}

as it would ensure that the $\textsc{P}{RIMAL}$ objective never exceeds the $\textsc{D}{UAL}$ by more than $\alpha$.

Thus, we need to deisgn an algorithm for the primal such that 
\begin{align*}
\Delta \textsc{P}{RIMAL} \le \begin{cases}\alpha &\text{if } i \le B \\ \emptyset &\text{if } i > B\end{cases}
\end{align*}

Thus, when $i > B$, we need $x_i = x_{i - 1} = 0$. As an aside, if $x_{i - 1}$, the constraint $z + x_{i - 1} \ge 1$ implies that we have already set $z = 1$ at time $i - 1$.

Now, we also want $\Delta \textsc{P}{RIMAL} \le \alpha$ when $i \le B$. First, note that the algorithm we're designing sets some value $x_i$ if the ski season lasts for $\ge i$ days. For convenience, we call this value $f\left(\frac{i}{B}\right)$ where $f:[0, 1] \to [0, 1]$. Then, 

\begin{align*}
\alpha \ge \Delta \textsc{P}{RIMAL} &= x_i + B(x_{i - 1} - x_{i}) \\
&= f\left(\frac{i}{B}\right) - \frac{f\left(\frac{i}{B}\right) - f\left(\frac{i - 1}{B}\right)}{\frac{1}{B}}
\end{align*}

What if we consider the solution to the following differential equation? 

\begin{align*}
f(x) - f'(x) = \alpha \\
\implies \frac{f'(x)}{f(x) - \alpha} = 1 \\
\implies \int \frac{f'(x)}{f(x) - \alpha} \, dx = x + C \\
\end{align*}

As we claimed prior $\alpha > 1$ and so, with $A = e^C$, we get
\begin{align*}
&\implies \ln [ \alpha - f(x) ] = x + C \\
&\implies \alpha - f(x) = A e^x \\
&\implies f(x) = \alpha - A e^x.
\end{align*}

